{"availability":"available","full":"almostimplemented/flattensf","name":"flattensf","description":"Explore this public GitHub project.","category":"Other","language":"HTML","stars":12,"forks":1,"created":"2026-09-16T22:38:49Z","updated":"2026-10-06T23:24:12Z","license":null,"branch":"claude/sf-flat-routes-analysis-e33in5","homepage":"https://almostimplemented.github.io/flattensf/","topics":[],"demo":"https://almostimplemented.github.io/flattensf/","color":"#63362a","ink":"#f8c7a8","lastCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:46.118Z","lastAttemptAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:46.118Z","lastAvailableAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:46.118Z","metrics":[{"at":"2026-10-07T02:54:46.118Z","stars":12,"forks":1}],"technologies":[],"overview":{"kind":"readme","version":1,"paragraphs":["flattensf.com: the flattest route between any two places in San Francisco, and every route between it and the shortest.","Behind it, an analysis of the city's hidden network of relatively flat streets: the routes that connect San Francisco's neighborhoods while climbing as little as possible.","San Francisco is famously hilly, but it is hilly in a structured way. Its hills are separated by valleys, saddles and old dune flats, and those low places join up into a network that is far more continuous than the city's reputation suggests. This project models that network from authoritative elevation and street data, routes across it under several competing definitions of \"flat\", and then works out which streets the city's geography forces low-gradient traffic onto."],"features":[],"sourceUrl":"https://github.com/almostimplemented/flattensf/blob/claude%2Fsf-flat-routes-analysis-e33in5/README.md","fingerprint":"b5c4220e14bb34792987946c3b9917628a150db5262602ae424252489db47b81","extractedAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:46.261Z"},"overviewCheckedAt":"2026-10-07T02:54:46.261Z","projectInsights":{"version":1,"whatItDoes":{"text":"flattensf.com: the flattest route between any two places in San Francisco, and every route between it and the shorte